    {
      "paperId": "/article/10.1007/s11214-014-0117-8",
      "doi": "10.1007/s11214-014-0117-8",
      "title": "The Solar-Stellar Connection",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "A. S. Brun, R. A. García, ... M. Pinsonneault"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2014,
      "abstract": "We discuss how recent advances in observations, theory and numerical simulations have allowed the stellar community to progress in its understanding of stellar convection, rotation and magnetism and to assess the degree to which the Sun and other stars share similar dynamical properties. Ensemble asteroseismology has become a reality with the advent of large time domain studies, especially from space missions. This new capability has provided improved constraints on stellar rotation and activity, over and above that obtained via traditional techniques such as spectropolarimetry or CaII H&K observations. New data and surveys covering large mass and age ranges have provided a wide parameter space to confront theories of stellar magnetism. These new empirical databases are complemented by theoretical advances and improved multi-D simulations of stellar dynamos. We trace these pathways through which a lucid and more detailed picture of magnetohydrodynamics of solar-like stars is beginning to emerge and discuss future prospects.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "We discuss how recent advances in observations, theory and numerical simulations have allowed the stellar community to progress in its understanding...",
